.TH PROCBENCH 1
.SH NAME
procbench \- what a Dis process and a channel cost
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B procbench
[
.B -n
.I entity-steps
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Procbench
measures the cost of doing a fixed amount of per-entity work three ways,
so that "one process per entity" can be a decision rather than a
preference.
.PP
.B /doc/games-plan.md
proposes a game whose swarm is one Dis process an entity, communicating
over channels, on the grounds that this is the obvious Inferno reading
of "many small independent things at once".
That reading might have been wrong: execution here is serialised, not
parallel, so N processes and their traffic could cost strictly more than
a loop and buy nothing but shape.
This says by how much.
.PP
The three arrangements all do the same arithmetic per entity per frame,
so what is measured is the machinery:
.TP
.B loop
one process, a loop over an array
.TP
.B procs
one process an entity, each woken on its own channel and waited for
.TP
.B pool
one process an entity, all taking from one channel and replying on one
.PP
.B Pool
is the arrangement a game would actually have, because
.B alt
cannot be written over an array of channels.
A bare channel round trip and a
.B spawn
are timed separately.
.PP
.I Entity-steps
is the work each measurement is given, four million by default.
Every measurement gets the same amount rather than the same number of
frames, because the clock is
.IR sys-millisec (2)
and a measurement lasting two milliseconds is two significant figures of
nothing.
The first version of this printed 0.00 for half the table.
.SH EXAMPLE
Numbers from this tree, on an Apple M-series host.
Compiled is
.BR "emu -c1" ,
interpreted is
.BR "emu -c0" ;
say which, because they are different code and emu interprets unless
asked.
.PP
.EX
                  compiled   interpreted
channel round trip    0.03 us       0.07 us
spawn                13.56 us       7.29 us

microseconds per entity per frame, compiled:

entities    loop   procs    pool   procs/loop
       1    0.01    0.05    0.05     3.7x
      16    0.01    0.08    0.10     6.4x
     256    0.01    0.21    0.35    15.8x
.EE
.SH RESULTS
A process per entity costs four to sixteen times a loop, per entity, and
the shared-channel arrangement a game would really use costs more again.
.PP
It does not matter at the sizes these games run at.
Two hundred and fifty-six entities as processes come to about
.B 54
microseconds a frame against
.B 3
for the loop, out of the sixteen thousand a sixtieth of a second allows.
The ratio is bad and the absolute cost is negligible, so the shape is
affordable and the argument for it is that it reads well, not that it
runs fast.
.PP
What is not free is
.BR spawn ,
at seven to fourteen microseconds.
Spawning a process per entity per frame would cost milliseconds and is
the thing to avoid; spawning once when a level is loaded costs nothing
worth counting.
.PP
Spawning is
.I cheaper
interpreted than compiled, which is the opposite of everything else
here.
Why has not been chased.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/procbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1),
.IR sys-millisec (2)
.PP
.B /doc/games-plan.md
.SH BUGS
It measures one host.
The ratios are what to carry away, not the absolute figures.
.PP
The entities do not touch each other, so nothing here measures what
locking or shared state would cost, and a real swarm has both.
.PP
The three arrangements are checked against each other and the run fails
if they disagree, so a transcription error in one of them cannot show up
as a timing difference instead of a wrong answer.
Nothing checks them against anything else: if all three are wrong in the
same way, the timings are still comparable and still meaningless.
